    overview: `## A high trek without the flights

Gosaikunda is the most reliable short trek in Nepal. There is no domestic flight to be cancelled and no restricted-area permit to arrange: you drive six or seven hours from Kathmandu to Dhunche, walk for six days, and finish at Sundarijal on the edge of the Kathmandu valley. For anyone with a week, it delivers genuine high-altitude scenery for a fraction of the cost and logistical risk of the Khumbu.

## The lakes

Gosaikunda is a chain of 108 alpine lakes at around 4,380 m, sacred to both Hindus and Buddhists. Hindu tradition holds that Shiva struck the mountain with his trident to create the water. During Janai Purnima each August, thousands of pilgrims walk up in a single weekend — extraordinary to witness, but not a quiet time to trek.

## Crossing the Laurebina La

From the lakes the trail climbs to the Laurebina La at 4,610 m, then makes a long descent into the Helambu region. This is the highest point of the trek and the section most affected by snow. Between December and February the pass is frequently impassable without winter equipment, and most groups turn back from the lakes instead.

## Combining with Langtang valley

Gosaikunda pairs naturally with the Langtang valley trek. The combined route runs 12–14 days: up the Langtang valley to Kyanjin Gompa, back down to Syabrubesi, then up to the lakes and out over the pass. It is the best way to see the region if you have two weeks rather than one.`,
