    primaryAnchor: "Everest Base Camp Trek",
    content: `## Why altitude sickness happens

At 5,364 m — the altitude of Everest Base Camp — each breath contains roughly half the oxygen of a breath at sea level. Your body compensates by breathing faster, producing more red blood cells and altering blood chemistry, but those adaptations take days. Altitude sickness is simply what happens when you climb faster than that process can keep up.

This is why fitness does not protect you. It is a rate problem, not a strength problem. In fifteen seasons at the Pheriche aid post, the trekkers I treated skewed young and fit — precisely because they could walk fast enough to outrun their own acclimatisation.

## The three conditions

**Acute Mountain Sickness (AMS)** is the mild, common form: headache plus nausea, fatigue, dizziness or disturbed sleep. Above 4,000 m, a majority of trekkers experience some of it. It is not dangerous in itself, and it resolves with a rest day at the same altitude.

**High Altitude Cerebral Edema (HACE)** is swelling of the brain. The defining signs are confusion and ataxia — loss of coordination. If someone cannot walk a straight line heel-to-toe, that is HACE until proven otherwise.

**High Altitude Pulmonary Edema (HAPE)** is fluid in the lungs. The signs are breathlessness at rest, a persistent dry cough that may turn pink and frothy, and extreme fatigue. HAPE can develop in someone with no AMS symptoms at all.

Both HACE and HAPE are lethal within hours and both improve dramatically with descent.

## The rules that actually work

1. **Limit sleeping-altitude gain to 300–500 m per day above 3,000 m.** Your daytime high point matters far less than where you sleep.
2. **Take a rest day every 1,000 m.** On the Everest trail these are at Namche (3,440 m) and Dingboche (4,410 m). Neither is optional.
3. **Climb high, sleep low.** Rest days should include a hike a few hundred metres above your lodge, returning to sleep lower. This is more effective than sitting still.
4. **Drink 3–4 litres a day.** Dehydration mimics and worsens AMS.
5. **Never ascend to sleep higher with AMS symptoms.** This is the single rule that prevents nearly every serious case.

## What to do at each stage

For mild AMS: stop ascending. Rest at your current altitude, hydrate, take paracetamol or ibuprofen for the headache. If you are better after 24 hours, continue. If you are not, descend 500 m.

For any sign of HACE or HAPE: descend immediately, by at least 500–1,000 m. Do not wait for morning. Supplementary oxygen and dexamethasone or nifedipine buy time, but descent is the only treatment.

## On the "just push through" instinct

Every season, someone summits Kala Patthar with a headache they should have stopped for. Most get away with it. The ones who do not are why the Himalayan Rescue Association staffs an aid post at Pheriche.

The altitude will still be there next year. Tell your guide about symptoms early — a good guide will thank you for it, and no reputable operator will ever push a trekker past their acclimatisation.`,
